<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    Passwords are the keys to everything else worth protecting, and most
    households rent their custody — a subscription, or a browser vault owned
    by an advertising company. It's an odd arrangement to have arrived at by
    default.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is a proper vault on your own machine, and it is genuinely sealed:
    somebody who steals the disk gets nothing.
  </p>
</section>

<%= about_screenshot("passwords") %>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What it does</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Findable in two keystrokes") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Search first, copy without ever displaying the secret on screen. The
        daily interaction is meant to be two seconds long.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Generates good ones") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Strong random passwords, or memorable passphrases when something has
        to be typed by a human on a television remote.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Holds the six-digit codes") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Two-factor codes live beside the password, so logging in stops
        depending on one particular person's phone being in the house.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "More than logins") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The wifi, the bank card, the sealed note nobody should read until
        they need to. Anything whose whole value is that it stays secret.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What "sealed" actually means</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    The secrets are encrypted with a key derived from your login password,
    which the server never stores. So a stolen disk, a leaked backup, or a
    household admin having a look through the database all come up with
    nothing readable. This is a stronger guarantee than the rest of the app
    makes, and it is deliberately reserved for the things that need it.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    The honest cost: lose the passphrase and the contents are gone,
    including for you. A guarantee with a back door isn't the guarantee.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Shared where it should be</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    The wifi password, the streaming login, the utility account — shared to
    the household deliberately, one item at a time, and revocable. Everything
    else stays yours. There's also provision for the thing nobody plans for:
    what happens when somebody who held the keys is no longer around.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Getting in and out</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    You can bring in what your current manager exports, and take everything
    out again the same way. A vault you can't leave is a vault you don't
    control, which would rather defeat the point of moving in the first
    place.
  </p>
</section>
